  "body": "Reviewing [input type=image](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#image-button-state-(type=image)) the bookkeeping details state:\r\n>The element's `value` attribute must be omitted.\r\n\r\nHowever when reviewing [4.11.3.4 Using the input element to define a command](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#using-the-input-element-to-define-a-command) it states:\r\n>If the type attribute is in one of the Submit Button, Reset Button, **Image Button**, or Button states, then the Label is the string given by the **value attribute**...\r\n\r\nTesting the following snippet in the HTML validator:\r\n```html\r\n<input type=\"image\" value=\"foo\">\r\n```\r\nThe validator throws an error stating it should not have a `value` attribute, as I'd expect per the element's bookkeeping details.\r\n\r\nHowever, testing today in Firefox, Safari and Chrome/Edge (dev), the snippet will render the input with a visible text string of \"foo\" when the `alt` attribute is missing.\r\n\r\nWith all that said, since the browsers all seem to handle the `value` and use it as a label for the input, were the preferred methods are absent, should using the attribute on this input type still be considered invalid?  \r\n\r\nIf it should remain invalid, should the content of 4.11.3.4 be updated to better reflect this?\r\n\r\nThanks\r\n",
